{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To find the maximum possible value of the greatest common divisor (gcd) of \\(13n + 8\\) and \\(5n + 3\\), we denote this gcd by \\(d\\). Therefore, \\(d\\) must divide both \\(13n + 8\\) and \\(5n + 3\\). This means that \\(d\\) must also divide any integer linear combination of these two numbers. Specifically, we can use the Euclidean algorithm to find such a linear combination.\n\nFirst, we express \\(13n + 8\\) in terms of \\(5n + 3\\):\n\\[\n13n + 8 = 2(5n + 3) + (13n + 8 - 10n - 6) = 2(5n + 3) + (3n + 2).\n\\]\nSo, \\(d\\) must divide \\(3n + 2\\). Now, we can express \\(5n + 3\\) in terms of \\(3n + 2\\):\n\\[\n5n + 3 = 1(3n + 2) + (2n + 1).\n\\]\nSo, \\(d\\) must divide \\(2n + 1\\). Next, we express \\(3n + 2\\) in terms of \\(2n + 1\\):\n\\[\n3n + 2 = 1(2n + 1) + (n + 1).\n\\]\nSo, \\(d\\) must divide \\(n + 1\\). Finally, we express \\(2n + 1\\) in terms of \\(n + 1\\):\n\\[\n2n + 1 = 2(n + 1) - 1.\n\\]\nSo, \\(d\\) must divide \\(-1\\), which means \\(d\\) must be a divisor of 1. Therefore, the only possible value for \\(d\\) is 1.\n\n","prefix_end":985,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,1477,279,7192,3204,897,315,279,12196,4185,49109,320,91289,8,315,17767,16,18,77,488,220,23,57758,323,17767,20,77,488,220,18,59,701,582,78064,419,44858,553,17767,67,59,568,15277,11,17767,67,57758,1969,21749,2176,17767,16,18,77,488,220,23,57758,323,17767,20,77,488,220,18,59,568,1096,3363,429,17767,67,57758,1969,1083,21749,894,7546,13482,10601,315,1493,1378,5109,13,44763,11,582,646,990,279,21963,50078,12111,311,1477,1741,264,13482,10601,382,5338,11,582,3158,17767,16,18,77,488,220,23,57758,304,3793,315,17767,20,77,488,220,18,59,982,59,9640,16,18,77,488,220,23,284,220,17,7,20,77,488,220,18,8,488,320,16,18,77,488,220,23,481,220,16,15,77,481,220,21,8,284,220,17,7,20,77,488,220,18,8,488,320,18,77,488,220,17,4292,59,921,4